The bars' own buttons are one control each, and the screen is none.

A screen reader landed first on an unnamed control the size of the window, on
Home and on the menu: the root's click that puts the keyboard away. It is a tap
gesture instead, which does the same and offers nothing to land on.

The button that opens the menu was a clickable node with no name around a node
named Menu, and the back arrows, the drawer's close button and an action in the
top bar had the same shape. Each says its name, as a button, on the node that
takes the press, and the glyph inside says nothing.

Every control said this way, the markup's pressables and the tabs included,
also says whether it holds the focus, so a switch, a keyboard and a screen
reader all find it as focusable.

